{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents adding, changing, or removing end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies in a Habeas project to draft requirements and commit them, once agreed under {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`, before writing implementation or test code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: I found no structural obligation in `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill`, `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` or `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`. Each clause says what the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to do, so each is a content obligation on a document. None prescribes a library, algorithm, signature, type, field or delegation through a named interface.\n\nI checked each candidate clause for a behavior-preserving alternative that would violate it:\n- \"MUST direct agents ... to draft requirements and commit them ... before writing implementation or test code\": any wording or layout of the skill that gives this direction satisfies it. Where the instruction sits, such as a different section, is discretionary.\n- \"MUST direct agents not to commit requirements or write implementation or test code until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\": this is the same kind of content obligation. Section placement and phrasing are free.\n- \"MUST direct agents ... to commit the agreed requirements of all groups in one commit before any implementation, leaving out uncommitted edits the user said do not belong to the task\": this is also the content of the directions. How the text is organized is free.\n\nThe only identifier-like element is the skill name `habeas-requirements`. The root `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` already carries `name: habeas-requirements` in its frontmatter, and it is the file the requirement's own :impl: option names. It has sections `Workflow`, `Writing chapters`, `Choosing references`, `What the review checks` and `Gotchas`.\n\nWhether the skill's text actually gives the required directions is a content-conformance question for the behavioral criterion. No implementation marker is missing.\n\nbehavioral: The subject requires the bundled `habeas-requirements` skill (`assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`) to direct agents to do three things:\n- For a change that adds, alters, or removes end-user-observable behavior no agreed requirement specifies, draft requirements.\n- Commit the requirements once they are agreed.\n- Do both before writing implementation or test code.\n\nThe project checks these skill directives by asserting phrases and workflow-step positions in the skill text. Both tests below are marked `{test}` for the subject.\n\n**Obligations and evidence**\n1. **Drafting is directed.**\n   - `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code]` finds the draft step through the phrase \"Write drafts\". It asserts that step comes before review, gate, commit and implement.\n   - The same test asserts the opening rule that every end-user-observable result must follow from an agreed requirement.\n2. **Commit comes after agreement and before any code.**\n   - The same test locates the Gate step (\"no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\").\n   - It locates the commit step (\"then commit it, all groups together, before any code\") and the \"Implement and test\" step.\n   - It asserts the strict order `draft < review < gate < commit < implement`.\n   - That covers the cited `.agreement` and `.commit` conditions as they apply to this requirement. The ordering assertion would fail if the commit moved after implementation, or if the gate were dropped or moved.\n3. **Scope covers adding, changing and removing, and excludes behavior an agreed requirement already specifies.**\n   - `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]` asserts the step-1 exemption wording. That wording covers a change that \"adds, alters, or removes no observable behavior\", and a case where \"an existing requirement already specifies it (most bug fixes)\".\n   - The same test asserts the skill description says to use the skill \"before changing code, tests, or requirement text\".\n   - It asserts that the grouping and review step is step 3.\n   - It asserts the sentence that every change to agreed text, including a rewording or removing a requirement, goes through steps 3 to 5 before it is committed.\n\nThe skill text I read matches these assertions. Step 2 drafts, step 4 holds the gate, step 5 commits all groups together before any code, and step 6 implements and tests.\n\nThe tests check the skill's directives, which is the observable behavior here. No obligation is left without a test, and no testing exemption was needed. I found no acknowledgments, and no marking gap.",
  "references": "1869e93b2fb32934934787f1b2d9b5582e09c00b67a606e38133c7b50fca1e61",
